# "Historical Importance of Rock Creek, Gettysburg National Military Park" (a National Park Service typescript of the park, undated in the text layer; its descriptive account cites the Gettysburg Compiler of 1906 and the Warren survey of 1868-69 and recommends restoring the creek to July 1863): second reading of the descriptive account (PDF pp. 13 and 15), workstream bt-web, 2026-11-15.
# The troop-movement chapters (PDF pp. 4-11) quote the Official Records and are covered by the local Official Records; PDF pp. 14 and 16-24 hold maps, plates and typescript pages whose text layer is unreadable (an OCR of them is queued behind the machine lock; owed). PDF pp. 13 and 15 were looked at as page images and every digit was read from the image: the table of elevations and depths on PDF pp. 13 and 15 (Hanover Road 344 feet to the Baltimore Pike 296 feet, the Warren survey) is read from the image only and is not extracted (its columns are scrambled in the text layer).
@ NPS-ROCK-CREEK
%basis the Comte de Paris, the Gettysburg Compiler of 1906 and the Warren survey of 1868-69, as the report names them
%images 13, 15

13 | woodlots | During the battle Rock Creek's banks were covered with woods and bristling with rocks (Comte de Paris) | - | 1863-07 (the report cites the Comte de Paris) | stated | The report says that during the battle of Gettysburg the banks of Rock Creek were covered with woods and, as the name implies, bristling with rocks, citing the Comte de Paris | =During the battle of Gettysburg its banks were covered with woods and, as the name implies, it was bristling with {{rooks|rocks}}. ({{Cotmpte|Compte}} de Paris, {{?0|90}}) ~S
13 | general | Until 1865 Rock Creek was a fairly good mill stream with mills along its banks; the mills decayed after the timber was removed | - | 1863 (mills along the banks; the creek a fair mill stream until 1865) | stated | The report says that until 1865 Rock Creek was considered a fairly good mill stream and that, because of the removal of timber along its course since then, it could not be depended upon for power, so that all the mills along its banks were allowed to fall into decay and ruin | =until 1865 {{Rook|Rock}} Creek was considered to be a fairly good mill stream, but due to the removal of timber along its course since that time it could not be depended upon for power, hence all the mills along its banks were allowed to fall into decay and ruin. ~S
13 | general | The dam at McAllister's Mill first broke in 1877, was repaired and was swept away in the high water of 1879 | - | !1877-1879 (the dam broke and was swept away; it therefore stood until then) | stated | The report says the dam at McAllister's Mill first broke in 1877, was repaired and in the high waters of 1879 was finally swept away | =Its present low water condition is due largely to {{idle|the}} breaking of the dam at McAllister's Mill. This dam first broke in the year 1877, was repaired, but during the high waters in 1879 was finally swept away. ~S
15 | general | McAllister's Dam, half a mile north of the Baltimore Pike, made a broad sheet of water half to three-quarters of a mile long, backed up beyond the Harrisburg Road bridge (Gettysburg Compiler of 1906) | - | before 1877 (the dam era, described in the Gettysburg Compiler of 1906) | stated | The report says that according to the Gettysburg Compiler of 1906 McAllister's Dam, about half a mile north of the Baltimore Pike, formed a broad sheet of water that stretched upstream one-half to three-quarters of a mile from the breast of the dam, and that the dammed water of Rock Creek reached beyond the bridge on the Harrisburg Road, at least three miles | =McAllister's Dam, located about one half a mile north of the Baltimore Pike formed a broad sheet of water that stretched upstream one-half to three-quarters of a mile from the breast of the dam. The backing of the water was noticeable for several miles up stream, as the dammed waters of Rock Creek reached beyond the bridge on the Harrisburg Road, a distance of three miles at least. ~S | @15: According to the Gettysburg Compiler of 1906
15 | general | South of the Hanover Road the water would have been above a man's knees and in many places above the waist; the creek always bank full | - | before 1877 (the dam era; the report's reasoning from marks on the banks) | hedged | The report says that at almost any point south of the Hanover Road the depth of the water would have reached above a man's knees and at many points above the waist line, the creek being always bank full according to the marks still in evidence on the banks | =At almost any point south of the Hanover Road the depth of the water would have reached above a man's knees and at many points above the waist {{linej|line;}} the creek was always bank full according to the marks still in evidence on the banks. ~S
15 | general | The Warren survey found Rock Creek fordable for all arms at almost all places except near McAllister's Dam, with sudden rises of up to 12 feet | - | ~1868-1869 (Warren survey) | stated | The report says that according to the Warren survey of 1868-1869 Rock Creek was fordable for all arms at almost all places except in the immediate vicinity of McAllister's Dam, but was subject to sudden rises, sometimes as great as 12 feet above the ordinary low water level | =According to the Warren Survey, {{18681869|1868-1869}}, Rock Creek was fordable for all arms at almost all places, except in the immediate vicinity of McAllister's Dam, but this creek like all others in the county was subject to sudden rises, sometimes as great as 12 feet above the ordinary low water level. ~S
15 | general | At the foot of the northeastern slope of Culp's Hill Rock Creek divides into two channels forming an elongated island, each channel with a fall of 4 feet from 336 to 332 feet and 2 feet deep at low water (Warren survey map) | - | ~1868-1869 (Warren survey map) | stated | The report says that at the foot of the northeastern slope of Culp's Hill the bed of Rock Creek divides into two channels forming an elongated island and that, according to the Warren survey map, the creek there had a fall of 4 feet in both channels, from 336 feet to 332 feet, with a depth of 2 feet at low water | At the foot of the northeastern slope of Culp's Hill the bed of Rock Creek divides into two channels forming an elongated island. | The creek at this point according to the Warren Survey'Map had a fall of 4 feet in both channels, from 336 feet to 332 feet’, with a depth of 2 feet at the low water level
15 | general | The report recommends restoring the creek to its July 1863 condition, including McAllister's Dam below McAllister's Woods, to raise the water upstream | - | 1863-07 (implied: the dam is to be restored as part of the conditions of July 1863) | hedged | The report recommends a project to restore the whole area to its condition in July 1863 and that McAllister's Dam, just below McAllister's Woods, be restored, impounding the water and raising the stream level a considerable distance upstream | =It is recommended that in the near future a project for the restoration of this entire area to conditions existing in July, {{I863|1863}}, be undertaken, {{end|and}} that the dam known as McAllister's Dam, located just below McAllister's Woods, be restored, thereby impounding the water above the dam and raising the water level of the stream a considerable distance upstream. ~S
